      "math_academy_id": "q-333189",
      "sequence_position": 12,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?",
      "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/df61bf017441f2dc3e34253d89f98f42cce2ec1b27131b9204952b059e335af8.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-5t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}=\\frac{-5{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}}{-3{c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}}=\\frac{5{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/11484704b873e4f4cb449e8eb452e0e4e2ceb71d9f2918b052d5f18cbdd7ca7b.png)",
